    <div class="space-y-3 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">Why choose CJC-1295 without DAC?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">Choice depends on research objective. No DAC = ~30 min half-life = discrete physiological GH pulse. With DAC = ~8 day half-life = sustained GH/IGF-1 elevation. Use no DAC for pulsatile GH studies; use DAC for chronic activation.</p></details>
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">Is CJC-1295 (no DAC) the same as Mod GRF 1-29?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">Essentially yes. Both refer to the same 30-amino acid GHRH analogue with four substitutions but without the DAC linker. Both terms describe the short-acting, non-albumin-binding variant.</p></details>
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">What is the optimal partner peptide?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">Ipamorelin is preferred for pulsatile protocols: CJC-1295 provides GHRH signal, Ipamorelin suppresses somatostatin tone → synergistic, clean GH pulse. GHRP-2 can be used when maximum pulse amplitude is preferred over selectivity.</p></details>
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">What is the half-life difference vs DAC variant?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">No DAC: ~30 minutes → discrete GH pulse. With DAC: ~8 days (via albumin conjugation). This ~400-fold difference is entirely due to the maleimidopropionyl DAC linker.</p></details>
